1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,381
Kivona. Another night without power.

2
00:00:03,681 --> 00:00:05,303
Resident: Again?! The third time this week!

3
00:00:06,803 --> 00:00:12,456
Clinics and shops run on diesel generators: expensive, noisy and polluting.

4
00:00:13,506 --> 00:00:18,882
Minister Kerbu: Today Kivona signs with SunRiver Power. One hundred megawatts of light for our people!

5
00:00:19,932 --> 00:00:21,831
Emson Anahct: We are ready to build, Minister.

6
00:00:22,881 --> 00:00:24,908
Emson Anahct: Signed! Now we can raise the money.

7
00:00:25,208 --> 00:00:29,026
Tidianie Eugom: An MoU is a good start, Emson. It is not financing.

8
00:00:30,476 --> 00:00:33,185
A one hundred megawatt solar project.

9
00:00:35,135 --> 00:00:36,927
A signed MoU.

10
00:00:38,877 --> 00:00:41,074
A developer ready to build.

11
00:00:43,024 --> 00:00:44,688
Everything looks promising.

12
00:00:46,838 --> 00:00:49,057
But is the project really ready?

13
00:00:52,607 --> 00:00:56,212
Bankable is not enough. Episode one: The Signing.

14
00:00:59,462 --> 00:01:03,430
Six people look at the same deal. Each one sees a different risk.

15
00:01:04,030 --> 00:01:07,145
The Minister of Energy: political commitment.

16
00:01:07,595 --> 00:01:11,243
Public Debt, at the Ministry of Finance: fiscal risk.

17
00:01:11,693 --> 00:01:13,954
The utility: offtaker risk.

18
00:01:14,404 --> 00:01:16,921
The developer: project bankability.

19
00:01:17,371 --> 00:01:19,185
The lender: debt service.

20
00:01:19,635 --> 00:01:22,493
And the analyst, who reads the fine print.

21
00:01:24,293 --> 00:01:25,936
Watch for three signals.

22
00:01:26,436 --> 00:01:27,865
Blue is a fact.

23
00:01:28,465 --> 00:01:30,214
Yellow is a decision.

24
00:01:30,814 --> 00:01:34,206
Red is the red team: what could make the deal fail.

25
00:01:36,156 --> 00:01:40,380
When you see stop, pause the video, and answer before you continue.

26
00:01:42,930 --> 00:01:44,637
Part one. The promise.

27
00:01:47,237 --> 00:01:52,656
An MoU is a promise. Financial close is where the promise gets paid for.

28
00:01:54,406 --> 00:01:59,121
Across Africa, many power MoUs never reach financial close.

29
00:02:01,771 --> 00:02:05,803
Those that do are only as strong as the contracts behind them.

30
00:02:08,303 --> 00:02:18,714
Emson Anahct: Minister, lenders will need a bankable revenue contract and adequate protection against risks that could interrupt debt service. For this project, three things.

31
00:02:20,214 --> 00:02:34,315
Emson Anahct: One: a long-term PPA. Twenty years or more of guaranteed purchase. Two: a dollar tariff, so the lenders carry no currency risk. Three: a State guarantee, in case the utility cannot pay.

32
00:02:35,365 --> 00:02:37,989
Minister Kerbu: Give him what he needs. Our people want light now!

33
00:02:39,039 --> 00:02:41,514
Minister Kerbu: Paul? The PPA must be approved by Friday.

34
00:02:42,564 --> 00:02:45,913
Paul Ahmak: Friday? Minister, we have not even seen the grid study.

35
00:02:47,663 --> 00:02:50,991
Political calendars move faster than contracts.

36
00:02:53,441 --> 00:02:54,124
Stop.

37
00:02:54,624 --> 00:02:57,077
You are at the Ministry of Finance.

38
00:02:57,577 --> 00:03:00,670
The PPA must be approved by Friday.

39
00:03:01,070 --> 00:03:03,332
Nobody has seen the grid study.

40
00:03:03,732 --> 00:03:06,356
The tariff is in U.S. dollars.

41
00:03:06,756 --> 00:03:09,294
A State guarantee is requested.

42
00:03:09,694 --> 00:03:10,910
Would you sign?

43
00:03:17,110 --> 00:03:19,180
Let's see what you should check first.

44
00:03:21,530 --> 00:03:24,495
Test one. Can the utility actually pay?

45
00:03:27,095 --> 00:03:35,202
KivoElec. For every one hundred billed, seventy are collected, and ninety-five are needed to pay costs and IPPs.

46
00:03:36,702 --> 00:03:39,006
Paul Ahmak: We already pay some of our suppliers late.

47
00:03:39,306 --> 00:03:41,290
Belpau: So if we miss a payment to SunRiver...

48
00:03:41,590 --> 00:03:45,537
Paul Ahmak: ...the State guarantee is called, and the bill goes to the Treasury.

49
00:03:47,287 --> 00:03:48,460
Weak collection.

50
00:03:49,110 --> 00:03:50,262
Payment risk.

51
00:03:50,912 --> 00:03:52,832
The guarantee is called.

52
00:03:53,482 --> 00:03:55,018
And the Treasury pays.

53
00:03:57,318 --> 00:04:01,371
Decision note. The question: can the utility actually pay?

54
00:04:02,271 --> 00:04:05,877
The evidence. Seventy collected for every hundred billed.

55
00:04:06,327 --> 00:04:07,991
Ninety-five needed.

56
00:04:08,441 --> 00:04:10,766
Suppliers already paid late.

57
00:04:11,216 --> 00:04:15,867
The decision: don't sign until payment capacity is demonstrated.

58
00:04:17,067 --> 00:04:20,629
The strongest guarantee is a utility that can pay.

59
00:04:23,379 --> 00:04:26,280
Test two. Can the system absorb the power?

60
00:04:28,880 --> 00:04:31,973
Paul Ahmak: Belpau, the Ministry wants our comments by Friday.

61
00:04:32,273 --> 00:04:34,385
Belpau: Friday?! It is three hundred pages!

62
00:04:35,885 --> 00:04:39,789
Belpau: We pay for what the plant can produce... even when our grid cannot take it?

63
00:04:41,289 --> 00:04:48,905
Clause seven point two. The Offtaker shall pay for the Available Capacity, whether or not the energy is dispatched.

64
00:04:50,655 --> 00:04:53,002
The plant: one hundred megawatts.

65
00:04:55,252 --> 00:04:58,047
What the grid can absorb today: sixty.

66
00:05:00,297 --> 00:05:02,942
Forty megawatts. Paid, never used.

67
00:05:05,842 --> 00:05:10,983
Paul Ahmak: And the line upgrade we need for the full one hundred megawatts is not financed yet.

68
00:05:12,733 --> 00:05:17,234
Decision note. Can the grid absorb the contracted electricity?

69
00:05:18,134 --> 00:05:20,481
Sixty of one hundred megawatts.

70
00:05:20,931 --> 00:05:23,534
The line upgrade is not financed.

71
00:05:23,984 --> 00:05:28,442
And take or pay means the capacity is paid, dispatched or not.

72
00:05:28,892 --> 00:05:31,687
The decision: phase the plant with the grid.

73
00:05:32,887 --> 00:05:39,564
Take or pay protects lenders and is not bad in itself. Signed before the grid is ready, it bites.

74
00:05:42,314 --> 00:05:44,618
Test three. Who carries the risk?

75
00:05:47,218 --> 00:05:51,015
Tidianie Eugom: From our side, the project is bankable. Our loan is protected.

76
00:05:52,065 --> 00:05:53,452
Enilec Mok: Protected by whom?

77
00:05:54,502 --> 00:05:55,739
Tidianie Eugom: Well... by you.

78
00:05:57,189 --> 00:05:58,448
Tidianie Eugom: Lenders price risk.

79
00:06:00,398 --> 00:06:02,745
Tidianie Eugom: When the State carries it, the deal looks cheap.

80
00:06:04,695 --> 00:06:06,700
Tidianie Eugom: The cost is simply hidden elsewhere.

81
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:14,427
Enilec Mok: Tariff in dollars, revenue in local currency. If our currency falls, who pays the difference?

82
00:06:14,727 --> 00:06:15,601
Belpau: The Treasury.

83
00:06:17,351 --> 00:06:21,404
The PPA tariff: ten US cents per kilowatt-hour.

84
00:06:23,754 --> 00:06:29,685
Year one. One dollar buys six hundred Kivona francs. The tariff costs sixty francs.

85
00:06:32,035 --> 00:06:37,539
Year five. One dollar buys eight hundred francs. The same tariff costs eighty.

86
00:06:39,689 --> 00:06:41,246
Same dollar tariff.

87
00:06:43,296 --> 00:06:45,685
One third more in local money.

88
00:06:48,235 --> 00:06:50,219
Who carries the currency risk?

89
00:06:53,869 --> 00:06:57,794
In this deal: the utility. And behind it, the Treasury.

90
00:07:00,544 --> 00:07:03,787
Test four. Can the public sector live with the deal?

91
00:07:06,387 --> 00:07:09,075
Enilec Mok: A State guarantee? That is not in our budget.

92
00:07:09,375 --> 00:07:11,935
Belpau: Not yet. It is a contingent liability.

93
00:07:13,435 --> 00:07:24,080
Enilec Mok: A guarantee needs almost no cash on the day we sign. But the contingent exposure starts when the guarantee is issued, and it can carry fees. That is exactly why it is dangerous.

94
00:07:25,580 --> 00:07:29,100
What the signing ceremony shows: a bankable project.

95
00:07:29,400 --> 00:07:36,184
What the Treasury may pay later: currency risk. Take or pay. The State guarantee. Utility arrears.

96
00:07:37,934 --> 00:07:42,755
Decision note. What is the maximum, and the expected, fiscal exposure?

97
00:07:43,655 --> 00:07:46,301
A State guarantee with no ceiling.

98
00:07:46,751 --> 00:07:48,585
Take or pay commitments.

99
00:07:49,035 --> 00:07:50,294
Currency risk.

100
00:07:50,744 --> 00:07:52,387
Utility arrears.

101
00:07:52,837 --> 00:08:00,069
The decision: identify, measure and disclose every contingent liability. Before signing, not after.

102
00:08:02,669 --> 00:08:05,677
Enilec Mok: Around this table, who defends the country's balance sheet?

103
00:08:06,727 --> 00:08:08,967
Belpau: To be frank, nobody trained us for this.

104
00:08:10,417 --> 00:08:11,100
Stop.

105
00:08:11,600 --> 00:08:14,245
You are the Director of Public Debt.

106
00:08:14,745 --> 00:08:17,412
The lender says the loan is protected.

107
00:08:17,812 --> 00:08:20,308
Protected by a State guarantee.

108
00:08:20,708 --> 00:08:23,118
The guarantee has no ceiling.

109
00:08:23,518 --> 00:08:25,417
It is not in the budget.

110
00:08:25,817 --> 00:08:27,289
Would you issue it?

111
00:08:33,489 --> 00:08:35,857
Now, let's put the deal under stress.

112
00:08:38,207 --> 00:08:39,551
The stress test.

113
00:08:42,401 --> 00:08:45,089
What if the currency falls forty percent?

114
00:08:47,539 --> 00:08:50,334
What if demand does not materialise?

115
00:08:52,784 --> 00:08:56,069
What if the cheaper hydro plant is dispatched first?

116
00:08:58,519 --> 00:09:02,828
What if the contract terminates in year three? What does the State owe?

117
00:09:05,278 --> 00:09:10,697
What if debt service coverage falls below the covenant in year five? Who moves first?

118
00:09:13,547 --> 00:09:16,235
Red team. What could make this deal fail?

119
00:09:17,035 --> 00:09:19,275
One. The offtaker cannot pay.

120
00:09:19,825 --> 00:09:22,321
Two. The grid cannot absorb the power.

121
00:09:22,871 --> 00:09:25,858
Three. The government carries hidden obligations.

122
00:09:26,408 --> 00:09:30,610
Don't ask whether the project is attractive. Ask what could break it.

123
00:09:33,060 --> 00:09:34,895
Minister Kerbu: Are you trying to kill my project?!

124
00:09:35,195 --> 00:09:37,349
Enilec Mok: No, Minister. We are trying to save it.

125
00:09:38,849 --> 00:09:48,897
Belpau: Three risks in this PPA. One: take or pay before the grid is ready. Two: full exposure to the dollar. Three: a State guarantee with no ceiling.

126
00:09:50,397 --> 00:09:55,346
Minister Kerbu: Call everyone: developer, lender, utility, Finance. Same table, next week.

127
00:09:56,846 --> 00:09:59,619
Instead of fighting the deal, they fixed it.

128
00:09:59,919 --> 00:10:01,498
Minister Kerbu: Let us fix this deal together.

129
00:10:02,548 --> 00:10:07,220
Paul Ahmak: First, we fix our cash flow: meters, collection, and a payment reserve.

130
00:10:08,270 --> 00:10:12,729
Belpau: We phase the plant with the grid upgrade. No paying for power we cannot use.

131
00:10:13,779 --> 00:10:19,667
Tidianie Eugom: We can add a partial risk guarantee and a local currency loan. Less risk stays with the State.

132
00:10:20,717 --> 00:10:25,346
Enilec Mok: And every guarantee goes into a register, with a ceiling and a yearly risk report.

133
00:10:26,846 --> 00:10:30,473
Emson Anahct: A deal that breaks my client breaks me too. Count me in.

134
00:10:30,773 --> 00:10:31,733
Minister Kerbu: Then we have a deal.

135
00:10:33,233 --> 00:10:47,676
The new deal. Sixty megawatts now, forty when the grid is ready. A local currency loan for part of the debt. A capped guarantee, plus a partial risk guarantee. A payment reserve. And every commitment in a risk register.

136
00:10:47,976 --> 00:10:52,285
Same plant. Same developer. Same lenders. A different deal.

137
00:10:54,035 --> 00:10:56,275
The signing test. Should we sign?

138
00:10:58,875 --> 00:11:03,888
A deal can be bankable for the project, and still unsustainable for the country.

139
00:11:05,338 --> 00:11:07,855
Bankable: lenders can finance it.

140
00:11:08,455 --> 00:11:12,338
Affordable: the utility and consumers can carry the cost.

141
00:11:12,938 --> 00:11:16,799
Executable: the grid and institutions can deliver it.

142
00:11:17,399 --> 00:11:21,154
Sustainable: public obligations remain manageable.

143
00:11:21,754 --> 00:11:24,549
Bankable is not the same as sustainable.

144
00:11:25,749 --> 00:11:27,690
Bankable is not enough.

145
00:11:30,740 --> 00:11:33,577
Before you sign, answer five questions.

146
00:11:34,177 --> 00:11:36,673
One. Can the utility actually pay?

147
00:11:37,473 --> 00:11:40,929
Two. What exactly does the PPA require us to pay?

148
00:11:41,729 --> 00:11:45,505
Three. Can the grid absorb the contracted electricity?

149
00:11:46,305 --> 00:11:48,567
Four. Who carries the currency risk?

150
00:11:49,367 --> 00:11:53,740
Five. What happens if the project fails, or the contract terminates?

151
00:11:54,540 --> 00:11:58,295
If you cannot answer these questions, you are not ready to sign.

152
00:12:01,495 --> 00:12:04,012
Months later. Financial close.

153
00:12:04,312 --> 00:12:06,211
Minister Kerbu: This time, we sign a deal we can keep.

154
00:12:07,711 --> 00:12:09,930
Enilec Mok: Every guarantee: recorded and capped.

155
00:12:11,430 --> 00:12:14,203
Belpau: Bankable is the start. Sustainable is the goal.

156
00:12:15,653 --> 00:12:17,594
Bankable is not enough.

157
00:12:18,494 --> 00:12:20,692
Before you sign, test the deal.

158
00:12:21,792 --> 00:12:24,842
Bankability is the beginning. Not the end.

159
00:12:28,492 --> 00:12:35,425
The ten questions, and the full transaction toolkit, are in the book. Bankable is not enough, episode one.
